---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import SlopeCalculator from '@/components/tools/calc2/SlopeCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="slope-calculator">
  <SlopeCalculator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the slope calculator works</h2>
    <p>
      The slope calculator divides the rise (the change in y) by the run (the change in x) between your
      two points. From the slope and one point it writes the equation of the line in three forms.
    </p>
    <pre><code>slope            m = (y₂ − y₁) ÷ (x₂ − x₁)
y-intercept      b = y₁ − m x₁
slope-intercept  y = m x + b
point-slope      y − y₁ = m (x − x₁)
standard         A x + B y = C, with A = y₂ − y₁, B = x₁ − x₂, C = A x₁ + B y₁
angle            θ = arctan(m)</code></pre>

    <h2>Worked example: the slope between (1, 2) and (3, 8)</h2>
    <pre><code>m = (8 − 2) ÷ (3 − 1) = 6 ÷ 2 = 3
b = 2 − 3 × 1 = −1
y = 3x − 1        y − 2 = 3(x − 1)        3x − y = 1
θ = arctan 3 = 71.57°</code></pre>
    <p>
      When the coordinates have few decimals, the slope is also shown as a reduced fraction, and the
      standard form uses whole numbers with no common factor.
    </p>

    <h2>Reading a slope</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Positive: the line rises from left to right. Negative: it falls.</li>
      <li>0: a horizontal line. Undefined: a vertical line, because the run is zero.</li>
      <li>Two lines are parallel when their slopes are equal, and perpendicular when the slopes multiply to −1.</li>
      <li>A grade in percent is the slope × 100: a slope of 0.08 is an 8% road grade.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <p>
      The calculator works with straight lines through two distinct points. For a curve, the slope changes
      from point to point; the slope between two points on it is the average rate of change between them.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
